Chapter 14
The capital budget, and its relevant cash flows
الموازنة الرأسمالية وتدفقاتها النقدية الملائمة
Book 2 ended with Orontes holding $7,302,100 of cash and two projects waiting: a roasting line and a warehouse, returning 14 and 11 per cent against a cost of capital of 9.04. Chapter 8 of that book said the budget stops where the projects cross the cost of the money. It did not say how the 14 per cent was computed.
This chapter and the three that follow answer that. Capital budgeting is the analysis of investments whose effects run for years, and it differs from everything in Chapters 1 to 13 in two ways: the amounts are large and irreversible, and the timing matters, because a dollar in year five is worth less than a dollar today. This chapter builds the cash flows. Chapters 16 and 17 discount them.
Figure F714-01 is your map.
Figure F714-01. Chapter 14 roadmap: the stages of a capital budget, and the three groups of cash flow it needs.
