WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 5 established that only future cash flows that differ between alternatives are relevant, and that sunk costs and allocated overheads are not. Chapter 9 added that tax scales every option alike unless the options are taxed differently. Book 2 put Orontes's cost of capital at 9.04 per cent and its tax rate at 25. All four are inputs to this chapter.
14.1  What capital budgeting is
Capital budgeting is the process by which a company identifies, evaluates, selects and controls investments whose benefits extend beyond one year. The outcome asks for the stages, and there are six.
Identification. Proposals arise from the strategy, from operations, from a regulatory requirement, or from a problem — a machine that is failing, a market that is growing, a rule that must be met. A company with no flow of proposals has a management problem rather than an investment problem.
Screening. A quick test against the strategy, the available funds and an obvious hurdle, to avoid evaluating in detail what will never be approved.
Evaluation. The analysis this chapter and the next three set out: estimate the cash flows, choose a discount rate, compute the measures, and test the result for risk.
Selection. Ranking the surviving proposals and fitting them to the funds available, which Book 2's Chapter 8 did with the marginal cost of capital schedule.
Implementation. Authorising, spending, and controlling the spend against the authority — because a project approved at $3,000,000 and delivered at $4,200,000 was a different project.
Review, or the postaudit. Comparing what happened with what was promised, which Chapter 15 treats in full.
Two features distinguish the whole process from the decisions of Chapters 5 to 9.
Irreversibility. A machine bought is hard to unbuy, and a plant built in the wrong place stays there. That is why the analysis is done before rather than after, and why Chapter 15 spends a section on real options.
Time. The money goes out now and comes back over years, so the arithmetic must account for the fact that money has a time value — which is why the measures of Chapters 16 and 17 discount.
