14.2  Cash flows, not accounting profits
Capital budgeting uses cash flows and not accounting profits, and the outcome asks why. Four reasons.
Cash is what can be reinvested, distributed or used to repay debt. Profit is an opinion about timing; cash is a fact about a bank account.
Accrual accounting deliberately spreads things. Revenue is recognised when earned rather than when received, and the cost of an asset is spread as depreciation over its life. Both are right for reporting a period and wrong for valuing a project, because the whole point is when the money moves.
Depreciation is not a cash flow at all. The cash moved when the asset was bought, which is the initial investment, and depreciation enters a capital budgeting analysis only through the tax it saves — the depreciation tax shield of section 14.4.
And the relevance rules of Chapter 5 apply unchanged. Only incremental cash flows count: those that would not exist without the project.
Five items recur and each is examined.
Sunk costs are excluded. Market research already paid for, a site survey, a feasibility study. The $25,000 of development in Chapter 6's contract was the same idea in a different setting.
Opportunity costs are included. If the project uses land the company already owns, the land's market value is a cost of the project, because selling it is the alternative. If it uses capacity that would otherwise make something else, the contribution forgone is a cost.
Allocated overhead is excluded unless it changes. A project charged a share of head office costs that do not move is being charged for something it does not cause.
Financing costs are excluded from the cash flows. Interest and the repayment of principal are not project cash flows; the cost of money is accounted for in the discount rate, and including it in both places charges for it twice. This is the single most common error in this topic.
Side effects are included. Cannibalisation of the company's existing products, as in Chapter 9, and any benefit to other products, both belong in the project's cash flows.
Figure F714-02 sorts them.
Figure F714-02. In or out of a project's cash flows
@table
Item | In? | Why
Incremental revenue and cash costs | Yes | They would not exist without the project
Depreciation | No, except through tax | The cash moved when the asset was bought
Interest and loan repayments | No | The cost of money is in the discount rate
Sunk costs already incurred | No | They fail the first relevance test
Opportunity cost of assets used | Yes | The alternative use is given up
Allocated head office overhead | No, unless it changes | The project does not cause it
Lost contribution on cannibalised sales | Yes | An incremental effect of the project
Working capital invested and recovered | Yes | Cash out and later cash in
@end
EXAM TRAP   Interest belongs in the rate, not the flows
Deducting interest from a project's cash flows and then discounting at the cost of capital charges for the money twice and will reject good projects.
Depreciation appears in the analysis only as a tax shield. It is added back if a question starts from accounting profit, and never deducted as a cost.
A question that gives you an accounting profit is asking you to convert it: add back depreciation, adjust for working capital movements, and remove any non-cash item.
