14.3  The three groups of cash flow
Every project's cash flows fall into three groups, and building them in that order is the discipline that prevents omissions.
The initial investment, at time zero. The purchase price of the asset, plus delivery, installation and commissioning; plus the initial investment in working capital; plus any opportunity cost of assets committed; less the after-tax proceeds of anything sold or displaced.
The operating cash flows, each year of the project's life. The incremental revenue less the incremental cash operating costs, taxed, plus the depreciation tax shield.
The terminal cash flow, in the final year. The after-tax proceeds of selling the asset, plus the recovery of the working capital, plus or less any closing costs.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: Orontes's roasting line
Step 1. The initial investment. The line costs $3,000,000 installed, and the extra stock and receivables it brings require $400,000 of working capital. The initial outlay is 3,000,000 + 400,000 = 3,400,000.
Step 2. The annual operating flows, before tax. The line adds $2,400,000 of revenue and $1,200,000 of cash operating costs, so the pre-tax operating cash flow is 2,400,000 − 1,200,000 = 1,200,000 a year.
Step 3. Tax on that. At 25 per cent the after-tax operating cash flow is 1,200,000 × 0.75 = 900,000.
Step 4. The depreciation tax shield. The line is depreciated straight-line over five years to nil: 3,000,000 ÷ 5 = 600,000 a year, which at 25 per cent saves 600,000 × 0.25 = 150,000 of tax a year.
Step 5. The annual total. 900,000 + 150,000 = 1,050,000 a year for five years.
Step 6. The terminal flow. The line would fetch $200,000 at the end, and since its book value is nil the whole proceeds are taxable: 200,000 × 0.75 = 150,000 after tax. The working capital of $400,000 is released. The terminal flow is 400,000 + 150,000 = 550,000, received in year five in addition to that year's operating flow.
Check. The project is $3,400,000 out and $1,050,000 a year in for five years, with $550,000 more at the end. Undiscounted that is 1,050,000 × 5 = 5,250,000 plus 550,000, or 5,800,000 — comfortably more than $3,400,000, but the comparison is meaningless until the timing is accounted for. Chapter 16 discounts it and finds a net present value of about 1,041,630 at 9 per cent, which is where the 14 per cent return quoted in Book 2 comes from.
Figure F714-03 sets the three groups out.
Figure F714-03. The roasting line's cash flows
@table
Item | Amount
Equipment, installed, at time zero | (3,000,000)
Working capital invested at time zero | (400,000)
Total initial investment | (3,400,000)
Pre-tax operating cash flow, each year | 1,200,000
After-tax operating cash flow, each year | 900,000
Depreciation tax shield, each year | 150,000
Annual cash flow, each of years 1 to 5 | 1,050,000
Sale proceeds in year five, before tax | 200,000
Tax on those proceeds | (50,000)
Working capital released in year five | 400,000
Terminal flow in year five, additional | 550,000
@end
