14.4  How tax reaches the cash flows
Tax enters a capital budgeting analysis in three places, and the outcome asks for all three.
On the operating flows. Every dollar of incremental pre-tax operating cash flow is taxed, so it is worth one less the rate: 1,200,000 × 0.75 = 900,000 in the example above.
Through the depreciation tax shield. Depreciation is not a cash cost, but it is deductible, so it reduces the tax paid. The shield is the depreciation times the tax rate, and it is a genuine cash inflow of the project. Where the tax rules allow accelerated depreciation, the shield arrives earlier and is worth more, which is why the tax code's depreciation method matters to a project's value even though the total deduction is the same.
On the disposal. Selling an asset for more than its tax book value produces a taxable gain; for less, a deductible loss. In the example the line's book value is nil, so the whole $200,000 is taxed: 200,000 × 0.25 = 50,000 of tax, leaving $150,000. Had the book value been $200,000 there would have been no tax at all, and had it been $500,000 the $300,000 loss would have saved 300,000 × 0.25 = 75,000.
Two further points appear in harder questions.
A company with losses has a marginal tax rate of nil until they are used, so its shields are worth nothing now and something later — which, as Book 2's Chapter 7 showed, is the standard reason a loss-making company leases rather than buys.
And an investment tax credit, where one exists, is a direct reduction of tax in the year of purchase and belongs in the initial investment rather than in the operating flows.
Figure F714-04 shows the shield's effect.
Figure F714-04. The same project, taxed and untaxed
@table
Line | No tax | At 25 per cent
Pre-tax operating cash flow | 1,200,000 | 1,200,000
Tax on it | — | (300,000)
Depreciation tax shield | — | 150,000
Annual cash flow | 1,200,000 | 1,050,000
Five-year total before terminal flows | 6,000,000 | 5,250,000
@end
Tax takes 12.5 per cent of this project's annual cash flow rather than 25, because half the deduction comes back as the shield. That is the practical point of the whole section: a 25 per cent tax rate does not reduce a project's value by a quarter.
