14.5  Working capital and inflation
Two refinements complete the cash flows, and both are examined in their own right.
Working capital. A project that adds sales adds receivables and inventory, and usually adds some payables. The net amount is an investment: cash goes out at the start, more may go out as the project grows, and the whole of it comes back at the end when the stock is sold and the receivables collected. Three rules follow.
The investment is a cash outflow when it is made and an inflow when it is recovered, and the recovery is usually assumed to happen in the final year.
Only the net change matters: an increase in payables funds part of the increase in receivables and inventory, exactly as Book 2's Chapter 12 described.
It is not depreciated and it generates no tax shield, because it is not consumed. The $400,000 in Orontes's roasting line goes out at time zero and comes back in year five undiminished.
Omitting working capital is a common error and it always flatters the project, because it removes an early outflow and a late inflow of equal size — and the early one is discounted less.
Inflation. Where prices and costs will rise, the analysis must be consistent, and there are two consistent ways to do it.
The nominal approach inflates the cash flows at the expected rate and discounts them at the nominal rate — the rate the market actually quotes, which already includes expected inflation, as Book 2's Chapter 3 showed through the Fisher effect.
The real approach leaves the cash flows in today's prices and discounts at a real rate, found by removing inflation from the nominal rate: at a nominal 9 per cent and 3 per cent inflation, 1.09 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0583, a real rate of 5.83 per cent.
Both give the same answer when applied correctly. The error the exam tests is mixing them: discounting cash flows stated in today's prices at a nominal rate understates every future inflow and rejects good projects, and it is the commonest inflation mistake in practice.
Two complications are worth naming. Not everything inflates at the same rate — sesame may rise faster than wages, and a contracted price may not rise at all — so a general inflation adjustment is a simplification. And the depreciation tax shield never inflates, because it is computed on the historical cost of the asset: in a period of inflation the shield loses real value every year, which is one of Book 1's points about historical cost, arriving in a project appraisal.
