Chapter summary
Figure F714-06 brings the chapter together.
Figure F714-06. Chapter 14 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Capital budgeting evaluates investments whose benefits run beyond a year, in six stages from identification to postaudit, and it differs from short-run decisions because its commitments are irreversible and its timing matters. It uses cash flows rather than profits: depreciation enters only as a tax shield, interest never enters at all because the cost of money is in the discount rate, sunk costs and unchanged allocations are excluded, and opportunity costs and side effects are included. The flows fall into three groups — an initial investment of 3,000,000 + 400,000 = 3,400,000, annual flows of 1,200,000 × 0.75 = 900,000 plus a shield of 600,000 × 0.25 = 150,000, giving 1,050,000, and a terminal flow of 400,000 + 150,000 = 550,000. Tax reaches the project in three places: on the operating flows, through the shield, and on the disposal, where proceeds of $200,000 against a nil book value leave 200,000 × 0.75 = 150,000 — and because half the deduction returns as the shield, a 25 per cent tax rate costs this project only 12.5 per cent of its annual flow. Working capital goes out at the start and returns at the end, is never depreciated and generates no shield, and omitting it always flatters a project. And inflation must be handled consistently: nominal flows at a nominal rate, or real flows at the real rate of 1.09 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0583, never one of each.
