Case-style practice: the warehouse proposal
Orontes's second project is a warehouse costing $2,600,000, with a ten-year life and straight-line depreciation to nil. It would save $520,000 a year of third-party storage and handling, and release $180,000 of working capital immediately by allowing stock to be consolidated. The site is land Orontes already owns, bought in 2009 for $300,000 and now worth $900,000. A feasibility study costing $45,000 has been completed. The company's tax rate is 25 per cent and its cost of capital 9.04 per cent. The finance director's draft shows an initial investment of $2,645,000 and annual savings of $520,000.
Required. Correct the draft, with figures, and state the annual cash flow the project should be credited with. Name the two items the draft has treated wrongly and the one it has omitted. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC14-1  Answer A. Beyond one year is the defining feature.
B is wrong. That is an operating decision.
C is wrong. Capital projects are uncertain.
D is wrong. Capital spending is capitalised, not expensed.
SC14-2  Answer A. Screening saves effort.
B is wrong. Screening is coarse and evaluation is not optional.
C is wrong. The rate comes from the cost of capital.
D is wrong. Payback is a measure, not a stage.
SC14-3  Answer A. The postaudit compares outcome with promise.
B is wrong. Screening happens first.
C is wrong. Authorisation permits the spending.
D is wrong. Identification generates proposals.
SC14-4  Answer A. The shield is the only cash effect.
B is wrong. No cash moves for depreciation.
C is wrong. The cash moved when the asset was bought.
D is wrong. The shield is real and must be counted.
SC14-5  Answer A. Counting it twice is the error.
B is wrong. That double-counts the cost of money.
C is wrong. Interest is not part of the asset's cost in this analysis.
D is wrong. Interest is not a terminal item.
SC14-6  Answer A. The alternative is to sell it.
B is wrong. Opportunity costs are included although no cash is paid.
C is wrong. Historical cost is sunk.
D is wrong. Book value is an accounting remnant.
SC14-7  Answer A. 3,000,000 + 400,000 = 3,400,000.
B is wrong. That omits the working capital.
C is wrong. This subtracts instead of adding.
D is wrong. That is the working capital alone.
SC14-8  Answer A. 1,200,000 × 0.75 = 900,000.
B is wrong. That ignores tax.
C is wrong. That is the tax.
D is wrong. This divides instead of multiplying.
SC14-9  Answer A. 600,000 × 0.25 = 150,000.
B is wrong. That is the depreciation itself, which is not a cash flow.
C is wrong. That is depreciation less the shield.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
SC14-10  Answer A. 200,000 × 0.75 = 150,000.
B is wrong. The gain is taxable.
C is wrong. That is the tax.
D is wrong. The proceeds are a real inflow.
SC14-11  Answer A. Earlier shields are worth more in present value.
B is wrong. The total deduction is the asset's cost either way.
C is wrong. Depreciation method does not change the asset's life.
D is wrong. The rate is set by the tax code.
SC14-12  Answer A. Out, then back, with no tax effect.
B is wrong. It is not consumed and is not depreciated.
C is wrong. Ignoring it flatters the project.
D is wrong. Recovering an investment is not taxable income.
SC14-13  Answer A. 1.09 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0583.
B is wrong. That is the subtraction approximation.
C is wrong. This multiplies the two rates.
D is wrong. That is inflation itself.
SC14-14  Answer A. Real flows discounted at a nominal rate are penalised twice.
B is wrong. The error reduces the computed value.
C is wrong. The two bases must match.
D is wrong. The effect is large over long lives.
