Case-style set: the warehouse proposal
The corrections. The draft's $2,645,000 includes the $45,000 feasibility study, which is sunk and must come out. It excludes the land, which is an opportunity cost of $900,000 — the company could sell it — and must go in. And it ignores the $180,000 of working capital released, which is an inflow at time zero rather than an outflow. The corrected initial investment is 2,600,000 + 900,000 = 3,500,000, less 180,000 of working capital released, which is 3,320,000.
The annual cash flow. The $520,000 of savings is a pre-tax cash flow, so after tax it is 520,000 × 0.75 = 390,000. Depreciation is 2,600,000 ÷ 10 = 260,000 a year, giving a shield of 260,000 × 0.25 = 65,000. The annual cash flow is therefore 390,000 + 65,000 = 455,000, not the $520,000 in the draft.
Note that the land is not depreciated and generates no shield, which is why only the building's cost enters the depreciation line.
The three errors, named. The two treated wrongly are the feasibility study, included although sunk, and the savings, taken before tax and without the shield. The one omitted is the land, which the company owns and which the project consumes: at $900,000 it is more than a third of the building's cost and is the largest single correction.
One further point for the board. The working capital release of $180,000 is an inflow now and will have to be reinvested if the warehouse is ever given up, so it should be shown as a recovery at the end of the project's life in the opposite direction — a detail the draft omits and which matters little at 9 per cent over ten years.
